## Slide 4: Success is defined before any model exists

**Headline:** Accuracy would reward a useless model, so we chose PR AUC first

**On the slide:**
- Big number: **<<f3(d1['valid_always_on_time_accuracy'])>>**, the accuracy of predicting "always on time" in September. It catches **0** delays.
- Metric set: PR AUC (main) · ROC AUC · Brier score · ECE (calibration) · lift = PR AUC / base rate · precision/recall at a threshold
- Random scoring gives PR AUC = base rate, so lift 1.0 means random.

**Decisions:**

<<dec(4, 6)>>

**Say:** "If we had used accuracy, the model that never predicts a delay would score <<f"{100 * d1['valid_always_on_time_accuracy']:.0f}">>%. PR AUC focuses on the delayed class. Our confidence intervals resample whole days, because flights on the same day share weather and are not independent."

**If asked:** *"What does lift <<f2(LR['lift'])>> mean?"* Logistic regression's PR AUC is <<f2(LR['lift'])>> times what random ranking would score on the same flights.

