## Slide 18: Why boosting gains little

**Headline:** Tree thresholds learned in summer sit in the wrong place in autumn

**On the slide (October, never the test set):**

<<md(["Feature groups (cumulative)", "Boosting, October PR AUC", "Logistic regression, October PR AUC"], [[a['features'], f3(a['october_pr_auc']), f3(g['calib_pr_auc'])] for a, g in zip(d5['ablation_october'], grp)])>>

- Top permutation importance: sched_hour <<d5['permutation_top12']['sched_hour']>>, h_flight_28d_rate <<d5['permutation_top12']['h_flight_28d_rate']>> (drop in PR AUC)
- Lift by slice: November boosting <<lift['November']['Gradient boosting']>> vs logistic regression <<lift['November']['Logistic regression']>>; JFK <<lift['origin JFK']['Gradient boosting']>> vs <<lift['origin JFK']['Logistic regression']>>; December ≈ <<lift['December']['Logistic regression']>> for all

**Visual:** `05_importance.png` + a line chart of the table (two lines diverging).

**Decisions:**

<<dec(34, 35)>>

**Say:** "When we add congestion and then history, boosting gets worse on October while logistic regression keeps improving. A tree learns rules like '28-day rate above 0.31' in a high-delay summer, and in a calm autumn every rate is lower, so the threshold is in the wrong place. A linear model only rescales the rate, so the level shift hurts it much less. That is drift, and it explains the headline result."

**If asked:** *"Gain vs permutation importance?"* Gain is loss reduction inside training and favours features with many split points. Permutation is the drop in held-out PR AUC when a feature is shuffled, which is what the model really relies on.

